use std
into ktx2.amni

Namespace ktx2; imports gfx.zlib and gfx.bc. Reads and writes KTX 2.0 texture files (the Khronos KTX File Format Specification version 2) holding one 2D picture with its mip levels, in RGBA8 or a block-compressed format. A format is the int kind the bc module uses (1, 3, 4, 5, 7 for BC1, BC3, BC4, BC5, BC7) with 0 meaning plain RGBA8, plus a flag for sRGB. All numbers in the file are little-endian. Nothing here may crash or read past the end on a damaged file: every offset and length is checked against the file's length before use, with the sums done in 64-bit ints so they cannot wrap.

The struct `Info` has, in this order: `ok` bool (default false), `kind` int, `srgb` bool, `width` int, `height` int, `levels` int, `scheme` int, and `error` int (default 0). Error codes are constants: `E_NONE = 0`, `E_SHORT = 1` (file too short), `E_MAGIC = 2` (not a KTX2 file), `E_FORMAT = 3` (a pixel format this module does not read), `E_SHAPE = 4` (3D, array, cube map, or a bad size or level count), `E_SCHEME = 5` (supercompression other than none or ZLIB), `E_LEVEL = 6` (a level's offset or length is wrong).

`level_size(kind int, w int, h int, n int) int` is the byte count of mip level n of a w by h picture: the level is `max(w >> n, 1)` by `max(h >> n, 1)`; kind 0 takes 4 bytes per pixel; the others are what the bc module's own `size` function gives for that level, called as bc::size(kind, level width, level height) (the module imports gfx.bc; no local copy of that function).

`is_ktx2(file) bool` (file: read-only byte slice) is true when the file is at least 12 bytes and starts with the bytes 0xAB, 0x4B, 0x54, 0x58, 0x20, 0x32, 0x30, 0xBB, 0x0D, 0x0A, 0x1A, 0x0A.

`head(file) Info` (file: read-only byte slice, which may be only the start of a larger file) parses the header and the level index without looking at the level data. After the 12-byte identifier come nine 32-bit fields: vkFormat, typeSize, pixelWidth, pixelHeight, pixelDepth, layerCount, faceCount, levelCount, supercompressionScheme; then the index: dfdByteOffset, dfdByteLength, kvdByteOffset, kvdByteLength (32 bits each), sgdByteOffset, sgdByteLength (64 bits each); that is 80 bytes; then one 24-byte entry per level, level 0 first: byteOffset, byteLength, uncompressedByteLength (64 bits each). Checks in this order, each failing with its error code and `ok` false: fewer than 80 bytes is E_SHORT; a wrong identifier is E_MAGIC; vkFormat must be one of 37 (RGBA8), 43 (RGBA8 sRGB), 131 or 133 (BC1), 132 or 134 (BC1 sRGB), 137 (BC3), 138 (BC3 sRGB), 139 (BC4), 141 (BC5), 145 (BC7), 146 (BC7 sRGB), else E_FORMAT; width and height from 1 to 16384, pixelDepth 0, layerCount 0, faceCount 1, and levelCount from 1 up to the number of levels a full chain has (1 plus the floor of log2 of the larger side), else E_SHAPE; scheme 0 or 3, else E_SCHEME; the file must hold the whole level index (else E_SHORT); and for every level the offset must not be negative, the length must be positive, and uncompressedByteLength must equal `level_size` for that level, and with scheme 0 byteLength must equal it too, else E_LEVEL. It does not check that the levels lie inside `file`. On success fill in every field with `ok` true.

`span(file, info Info, n int) int, int` (file: read-only byte slice, which may be only the start of the file) returns two ints, the byte offset and the stored byte length of level n, read from the level index in `file`. It returns -1 and 0 when `info.ok` is false, n is outside 0 to levels - 1, or `file` is shorter than 80 + 24 * (n + 1) bytes.

`read(file) Info` (file: read-only byte slice holding the whole file) is `head(file)` plus one more check when that is ok: for every level, the offset and length from `span` must not pass the end of the file (offset plus length at most the file length), else the result has `ok` false and error E_LEVEL. No header parsing here: `head` does it all.

`unpack(stored, info Info, n int, out) bool` (stored: read-only byte slice holding exactly the stored bytes of level n; out: growable byte array by reference) clears `out` and fills it with level n's bytes: false when `info.ok` is false or n is outside 0 to levels - 1. With scheme 0 it is a copy of `stored`, and false (with `out` empty) when its length differs from `level_size`. With scheme 3 `stored` is a zlib stream: false when it is shorter than 4 bytes; `zlib::inflate` it into `out`; false (and `out` cleared) when inflate fails, the result's length differs from `level_size`, or the stream's last four bytes (a big-endian Adler-32) differ from the Adler-32 of the inflated bytes (`zlib::inflate` does not check it). This block defines `adler32(data) int` (data: read-only byte slice) alone, the module's only checksum function: a starts at 1, b at 0; for each byte a = (a + byte) mod 65521, b = (b + a) mod 65521; the result is b * 65536 + a.

`level(file, info Info, n int, out) bool` (file: read-only byte slice holding the whole file; out: growable byte array by reference) clears `out`, takes level n's offset and length from `span`, and is false when the offset is negative or offset plus length passes the end of the file; otherwise it is the result of `unpack` on that part of the file. No decoding here: `unpack` does it all.

`write(kind int, srgb bool, w int, h int, levels int, data, deflate bool, out) bool` (data: read-only byte slice holding the levels back to back, level 0 first, each exactly `level_size` long; out: growable byte array by reference, cleared first) writes a complete KTX2 file; false (out left empty) when kind is not one of 0, 1, 3, 4, 5, 7, the sizes or level count would fail `head`'s checks, or `data` is shorter than the levels' total. Layout: the 12-byte identifier; vkFormat (kind 0: 37 or 43 with srgb; 1: 131 or 132; 3: 137 or 138; 4: 139; 5: 141; 7: 145 or 146; kinds 4 and 5 ignore srgb), typeSize 1, width, height, pixelDepth 0, layerCount 0, faceCount 1, levelCount, scheme (3 when `deflate`, else 0); the index with the Data Format Descriptor placed right after the level index (at byte 80 + 24 * levels), no key/value data (offset 0, length 0) and no supercompression global data (0, 0); the level index; the descriptor; then the level data stored smallest level first (the last level comes first in the file), each level's start padded with zero bytes to a multiple of 16 when the file is not deflated and of 1 when it is. The level index (in level order, level 0 first) records each level's offset, stored length and uncompressed length.
- When `deflate` is true each level is stored as a zlib stream made of stored blocks: the bytes 0x78, 0x01; then the data in chunks of at most 65535 bytes, each chunk preceded by one byte that is 1 for the last chunk and 0 otherwise, the chunk length as 16 bits and its ones' complement as 16 bits; then the Adler-32 checksum of the level's bytes, big-endian, from the `adler32` function the `unpack` block defines (call it; no checksum math here).
- The Data Format Descriptor: a 32-bit total size (4 + 24 + 16 per sample), then one basic block: a 32-bit 0 (vendor and type), 16-bit version 2, 16-bit block size (24 + 16 per sample), then single bytes: color model, primaries 1, transfer function (2 when the format is sRGB, else 1), flags 0, four texel block dimension bytes, eight bytes-per-plane bytes (the first is the bytes per block or pixel, the rest 0); then per sample 16 bytes: 16-bit bit offset, one byte bit length minus one, one byte channel id, four sample position bytes of 0, 32-bit lower limit 0, 32-bit upper limit.
- Kind 0: model 1, block dimensions 0, 0, 0, 0, plane bytes 4, four samples at bit offsets 0, 8, 16, 24, each 8 bits long with upper limit 255 and channel ids 0, 1, 2 and 15 (the alpha id becomes 31 when sRGB, marking it linear). The block-compressed kinds have block dimensions 3, 3, 0, 0 and upper limit 0xFFFFFFFF on every sample: kind 1 is model 128, plane bytes 8, one 64-bit sample with channel 0; kind 3 is model 130, plane bytes 16, two 64-bit samples: channel 15 at offset 0 then channel 0 at offset 64; kind 4 is model 131, plane bytes 8, one 64-bit sample with channel 0; kind 5 is model 132, plane bytes 16, two 64-bit samples: channel 0 at offset 0 and channel 1 at offset 64; kind 7 is model 134, plane bytes 16, one 128-bit sample with channel 0.
